From a5f6b4cbf67b868bc12f3841894652e68381610c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 19:14:50 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] Battery pricing engine, admin defaults, and load-flow
 fixes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Rates: one tariff table, not three
  Tide carried its own copy of the SDG&E tariff and it had drifted —
  winter peak read $0.354 against an actual $0.503, and the two
  "super off-peak" figures were NEM export avoided-cost values sitting
  in retail-rate fields. programData now derives its seasonal defaults
  from RATE_PLANS in the dispatch engine. Battery warranty terms
  likewise resolve through warrantyData rather than a second list.

Pricing engine (src/pricing/loanPricing.js)
  Contract -> federal -> AMOUNT FINANCED -> rebate -> net. The loan is
  sized before the rebate because the rebate arrives after funding.
  Adders, solar-panel tiered pricing, 20-year projection with a NEM
  cliff, and the rep commission floor all live here as pure functions,
  so the headline payment and the Breakdown accordion cannot disagree.

Admin defaults (src/admin/, api/settings.js)
  Market assumptions editable without a deploy: batteries, lenders and
  per-term rate cards, adders, panel pricing, incentive programs,
  utility rate tiers. Only CHANGES are stored, so a later fix to a code
  default still reaches anyone who has not overridden that field.
  Missing table is not an error on read — the app runs on shipped
  defaults until the SQL in api/settings.js is run.
  The account owner is always an admin; the unmatched case resolves to
  'client', which would otherwise silently demote the platform owner
  on a mistyped ADMIN_EMAILS.

Two bugs, same root cause: a figure read from the wrong source
  NEMStatusCard picked which kWh figure to show from the DOLLAR
  position rather than the ENERGY position. A house making 11,500
  against 10,500 is 1,000 kWh in surplus while still owing at true-up,
  so the card reached for `shortage` and printed 0. Verified: 791 kWh
  surplus rendered as 0.

  LoadSimulator projected from calculations.currentNEMImpact, which the
  engine had already recomputed WITH plannedAddedKwh fed from the
  simulator itself — then added the extra cost again. On a 3,500 kWh
  EV: $241 base + $1,404 extra should be $1,645, was showing ~$3,049.
  Now projects from baseNEMImpact.

Added load propagates
  One definition in BatteryModel places a planned load by its own
  daytime share rather than smearing it across the household curve —
  an EV charging at 1am must not read as absorbed by midday solar.
  Surfaced in the audit card, as a line over Consumption vs Production,
  and split out of both the true-up and the cost of doing nothing.

Eligibility fill estimate
  Replaced a flat 42% daytime-load haircut with hour-by-hour overlap
  against the selected consumption profile. The flat share was blind to
  WHEN load happens: an evening-heavy house in December was charged for
  11 kWh of midday load it does not have, landing at 4.32 kWh/day
  against a 7.78 threshold. Now 7.10, and the profile moves the answer.

