From 2d100995322b09554d7107e87b8a1cbff07c2286 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 12:04:12 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] Fix: cost of doing nothing never responded to added load
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The two-curve comparison added in the previous commit was dead code.
`loss` and `lossBase` were both computed from effExport/effImport, which
are the BASE figures — buildOverlayWithAddedLoad returns the with-added
values under separate keys and leaves the originals untouched. So the
delta was exactly zero on every client and the subline never rendered
once. The in-code comment asserting those figures "already carry the
load" was simply wrong.

The with-load export/import are now derived once in BatteryAnalysis and
passed down as props, rather than each section working them out for
itself. They are computed as a DELTA against whatever baseline is in
force, so they stay correct in manual mode and with measured Green
Button data — both of which describe the house as it is today, meaning
a planned EV still has to be layered on top.

The impact is not a footnote. On an 11,500/10,500 SDG&E client adding a
3,500 kWh EV, ten-year cost of doing nothing goes from $17,641 to
$52,536 — 3.0x. Both curves are now on the chart with the gap between
them exposed in red, because that gap is the argument.

Verified across overlay, manual and measured modes, zero load, a
12,000 kWh load (7.8x), a daytime pool pump (1.97x, correctly lower
than an overnight EV), an existing under-producer, and an
over-producer whose baseline is zero.

