From 4940f585c1231658260da0da04fb0229ae6b4af9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 20:05:27 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] The Beach, and the proposal reworked
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The Beach (rep centre)
  Three tides in the order a deal crosses them — Met, Project,
  Installed — and three panels answering the three questions a rep
  actually has.

  TREASURE splits banked from pipeline and refuses to blend them. A
  pipeline total spent in advance is how reps get hurt, so Met (priced,
  unsigned) and Project (signed, in build) are reported apart with the
  caveat stated rather than implied.

  SWELL is six months of proposals as bars plus a Met→Project→Installed
  funnel with the drop between each pair named. It says out loud that
  the ratios count where deals sit today, not cohorts, so a young book
  reads as converting worse than it does.

  QUIVER is an admin-editable rack with per-rep progress. It ships
  EMPTY — a training centre listing modules that don't exist teaches
  reps to ignore it.

  Commission comes from the figure already saved on each proposal, not
  a rate card, because Recruits carries only `Level` and no rates. When
  a rate table exists this panel reads it and the arithmetic is
  unchanged.

  api/beach.js falls through three sources for a deal's tide —
  Sales_Stage, then Project_Status (installed hardware outranks a sales
  stage), then the saved proposal — so The Beach works before
  Sales_Stage exists and keeps working after.

Proposal
  No hard-coded company anywhere. Seller and contractor are both data,
  stored on the proposal so an old one still renders correctly after
  the org changes installers. An unnamed seller renders a neutral
  placeholder rather than somebody else's brand.

  The 20-year graph is carried over from Battery Analysis, stacked the
  same way, driven by the rows saved WITH the proposal rather than a
  re-projection — the customer's chart cannot disagree with the
  appointment. Rep-only controls stay behind.

  Solar appears only when solar is being added, and leads with the
  consequential question: an exporting array on a grandfathered NEM
  1.0/2.0 tariff restarts the clock, which usually costs more than the
  extra production buys. Non-export keeps it. The verdict is computed,
  not asserted.

  Eight-step journey replacing the three. One list, two audiences:
  `actionable` marks what someone can do today, carries the links, and
  gates Converted to Project — waiting for Permission to Operate would
  leave every installed job sitting at Met for two months.

  Pricing breakdown moved below the steps, with term, rate and total of
  payments alongside it.

  Battery drawn in isometric SVG — no model file, no WebGL, nothing
  from a CDN, because it has to survive print, email and server-side
  render. Scales to real unit counts.

Note: vite build passed with a genuinely missing named export
(STEP_STATUS) that esbuild caught. Render tests are doing real work
that the production build is not.

