From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 20:26:38 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] Signing flow, intake, and the admin panels that were
 missing
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Closing a gap I left
  `parties` and `quiver` went into the settings schema last commit with
  no admin UI behind them — so the logo, contractor and training
  material had nowhere to be entered. Both panels now exist. Parties
  ships with both companies EMPTY: a default company name is what turns
  up six months later on a white-labelled proposal nobody meant to
  send.

Getting Started (src/project/ProjectSteps.jsx)
  The proposal answers "should I". This answers "how do I begin", and
  it is a different job — fewer words, one obvious action, visible
  progress so a half-finished signup does not feel abandoned.

  Every step is independently startable. A customer who cannot sign
  paperwork until a technician has visited will simply do nothing, so
  order is a recommendation, not a lock.

  Three actionable steps, five that happen to them afterwards shown as
  a timeline. Cash correctly drops Qualification and reads 0/2.

Intake
  Asks only what the homeowner knows and the contractor cannot find
  out without a phone call. Address, system, battery model and
  financing are attached automatically — a form that asks for what you
  already know teaches people to rush it. Fifteen questions across four
  groups, shaped as stable keys so the contractor's spreadsheet becomes
  a mapping in INTAKE_COLUMNS rather than a rebuild.

  Answers are STORED before they are emailed. An inbox is not a
  database, and when the sheet arrives the export reads from stored
  answers rather than from a sent message nobody can query.

The stage move is computed server-side
  api/project-steps.js reads the saved proposal and derives the stage
  from it; the request supplies one step id and a status, never the
  stage. A customer ticking boxes on their own phone is the normal path
  for this endpoint, and a client who can PUT themselves to "Converted
  to Project" is a CRM full of fiction.

  Access is deliberately wider than the other write endpoints — a
  client may update steps on their own contact, because doing it
  unattended is the point. They can do nothing else.

One mailer
  send-audit.js carried its own ZeptoMail and Resend senders. Intake
  needed mail too and a third copy is how a provider fallback quietly
  stops matching in one place. Extracted api/_mail.js; send-audit now
  uses it.

Test hygiene: the proposal leak check asserted the APR was absent.
It is now intentionally present — term and rate were requested in the
breakdown — so the assertion was narrowed to the genuinely rep-only
fields rather than deleted.

