From 1b8ad84873fb2e1d8f882c00e1b449bb26f80a92 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 20:43:54 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Aloha intake: the real form, with document uploads
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The previous intake was the wrong document
  It asked homeowners about roof age and gate codes. The contractor's
  workbook is a trade handoff carrying services, costs and the rep's
  commission — a different form for a different reader, and only this
  one gets a job built. Rewritten against their actual sheet.

The cost build-up, verified to the cent
  Total Project Cost = services + commission + loan fees
  Net Total          = total - PE prepaid - SDCP/SGIP

  Reproduced from the supplied Bean sheet: 11,880 + 12,300 + 1,000 =
  25,180; plus 9,134.12 commission; plus 2,650.875 loan fees =
  36,964.995. Less 10,457.39709 prepaid = 26,507.59791. Both match
  exactly.

  THE LOAN FEE IS CIRCULAR and their sheet does not fully converge. The
  fee is 10% of a base that is itself very nearly the Net Total, which
  depends on the fee. A clean solve gives 26,507.47; their sheet reads
  26,508.75, a $1.28 gap, and carries a 0.005 residual cell that looks
  like a goal-seek left mid-flight. So the base is an explicit editable
  field defaulting to the converged solve — a rep who must match their
  paper penny for penny types their number rather than reverse-
  engineering a circular reference under time pressure.

Pre-filled, not blank
  Panels, battery, adders, financing, commission, address and rebate
  all come from the saved proposal. Adders map onto the contractor's
  service lines (MPU → Service Upgrade, EV wiring → EV Charger,
  sub-panel → Circuit Relocation, non-export → Other Electrical).
  Retyping what the platform knows is how transcription errors reach a
  build order.

Documents, including the utility bill
  Four required uploads to a PRIVATE Supabase bucket, one folder per
  contact, read back through short-lived signed URLs. Private is the
  point: a utility bill carries a name, address, account number and
  consumption history, and a public bucket with guessable paths is a
  data leak wearing a convenience costume. Paths are always rebuilt
  from the contact id server-side, so a caller cannot request someone
  else's file by passing a full path.

Submission
  Generates the contractor's own workbook with exceljs — same section
  order, same row meanings, same wording, because a handoff that makes
  the receiving coordinator hunt gets bounced. Values not formulas:
  the arithmetic is verified once in intakeSchema and the sheet shows
  what the app computed, rather than a second implementation that
  could disagree.

  Emailed with every uploaded document to the contractor's submission
  addresses, which live on the contractor party in settings so a change
  of installer is a settings edit, not a deploy.

On commission appearing here: that is correct. Their form has a
Commission line and this packet is trade-facing. The rule is narrower
than "never show commission" — it is never on anything the CUSTOMER
sees, enforced and tested in CustomerProposal.

New dependency: exceljs. Requires npm install before deploy.

